Track 2: Which Larva Steers the Tug?
- Story & Context: Confined to the damp maintenance hold of Botlek dockyard, percussionist Joris wept into a pan of kerosene while tuning rosewood bars to match his estranged father’s failing respirator. He confessed into the open cargo-hold microphone that he had been secretly overriding harbor radio channels at dusk just to hear foreign vessels answer his mallet patterns across the foggy basin. His raw voice cracked into the foghorn's frequency as he pleaded: "Count the molted skins before the tide takes our names." Outside in the mist, two stranded oil tankers idled their engines, inadvertently whistling back in locked fifths.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Eerie, cinematic interior of an industrial ship hold filled with pooling black water and floating rosewood planks, faint green navigational lights piercing dense steam, moth cocoons clinging to rusted steel chains, minimalist and melancholic.

Track 3: Ballast Gain at Ecdysis
- Story & Context: Years after Pier 42 was condemned, dock-squatters claimed the entire tidal basin began singing autonomously whenever late-November mists covered the Maasvlakte breakwater. A salvage diver swore he watched thousands of migrating sand-beetles shed their shells in perfect unison across the sunken hull of the freighter Meridian, their carapace vibrations creating a subterranean wooden rumble that tripped the automated shore horns. The harbor beacons blinked blind warnings into the grey drift while the deep water harmonized with Vane's recorded voice: "We split the shell and let the deep water sing." Local skippers now cut their engines completely through the sound, drifting across the shoals in reverent silence.
